---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';

const updated = '10 September 2026';
---
<Layout
	title="Privacy Policy — ConfigButler"
	description="ConfigButler's privacy policy. We collect the minimum we need, we never sell or rent personal information, and you can have your data deleted by sending one email."
>
	<!-- HERO -->
	<section class="hero">
		<div class="hero-bg"></div>
		<div class="wrap" style="max-width:900px">
			<span class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span>privacy</span>
			<h1 class="display" style="max-width:14ch;margin-top:18px">
				Privacy <span class="b">policy.</span>
			</h1>
			<p class="lede" style="margin-top:22px;max-width:640px">
				We collect the least we can get away with, we use it only to do the thing you asked for, and
				we do not sell, rent, or trade personal information to anyone. Ever.
			</p>
			<div class="badges" style="margin-top:24px">
				<span class="pill pill-brass">built &amp; hosted in the EU</span>
			</div>
			<p class="mono" style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-3);margin-top:18px">
				last updated {updated}
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<div class="wrap"><div class="hr"></div></div>

	<!-- THE SHORT VERSION -->
	<section class="sect sect--paper-2 tight">
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="grid grid-4">
				<div class="f rv"><span class="fi"><Icon name="lock" /></span><div class="ft">Never sold</div><div class="fd">Your personal information is not sold, rented, traded, or shared for advertising.</div></div>
				<div class="f rv"><span class="fi"><Icon name="scan" /></span><div class="ft">No ad tracking</div><div class="fd">No advertising networks, no data brokers, no following you around the web. We measure to improve the product, never to sell you.</div></div>
				<div class="f rv"><span class="fi"><Icon name="doc" /></span><div class="ft">Minimum data</div><div class="fd">Your name, your email, and whatever you chose to tell us. That's the whole list.</div></div>
				<div class="f rv"><span class="fi"><Icon name="x" /></span><div class="ft">Deleted on request</div><div class="fd">Email us and your personal data goes away — bar the change history you asked us to keep.</div></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- THE POLICY -->
	<section class="sect">
		<div class="wrap wrap--narrow prose rv">
			<h2>Who we are</h2>
			<p>
				ConfigButler is a founder-led company run by Simon Koudijs, based in the Netherlands.
				For anything in this policy, write to
				<a class="link" href="mailto:simon@configbutler.ai">simon@configbutler.ai</a> — it reaches a
				person, not a ticket queue.
			</p>

			<h2>What we collect</h2>
			<p>There are exactly four ways we end up with your personal information.</p>
			<h3>If you contact us</h3>
			<p>
				The contact form takes your <strong>name</strong>, <strong>email address</strong>, and the
				<strong>message</strong> you write. Submissions are delivered to our inbox by
				<a class="link" href="https://web3forms.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Web3Forms</a>,
				which passes the message on and does not use it for anything else.
			</p>
			<h3>If you sign in with LinkedIn or GitHub</h3>
			<p>
				You sign in with an account you already have. Today that is <strong>LinkedIn</strong> or
				<strong>GitHub</strong>. Both use standard OAuth: you approve the request on the provider's own
				consent screen, we never see your password, and you can revoke our access from the provider
				whenever you want. On LinkedIn that is
				<strong>Settings &amp; Privacy → Data privacy → Permitted services</strong>; on GitHub it is
				<strong>Settings → Applications</strong>.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>LinkedIn</strong> gives us the basic profile fields you approve — your name, profile
				headline, profile picture, and email address. We use them to create your account and to know
				who is signed in. We do not post on your behalf, and we do not read your feed, your messages,
				or your connections. We do not ask for permission to.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>GitHub</strong> does two jobs. It signs you in the same way LinkedIn does. And, if you
				choose, it is how you hand ConfigButler access to one of your own repositories — which is the
				product: your repository becomes editable through an API, with every change committed back to
				it in your name. You grant that access per repository, you can see exactly which ones you have
				connected, and revoking it on GitHub cuts us off immediately.
			</p>
			<p>
				We ask for the narrowest access that makes a feature work, and nothing held "just in case". If
				something needs more, we will ask for it then rather than up front.
			</p>
			<h3>If you use ConfigButler to change configuration</h3>
			<p>
				Every change you make is recorded with your <strong>name</strong> and <strong>email
				address</strong>. That is the heart of what ConfigButler does, so it gets
				<a class="link" href="#your-repository">a section of its own</a> below.
			</p>

			<h3>If you just visit the site</h3>
			<p>
				This website is static and hosted on GitHub Pages, which keeps short-lived server logs
				(including IP addresses) for security and abuse prevention. Fonts are loaded from Google
				Fonts, so your browser's request reaches Google. We set no cookies of our own and run no
				analytics here.
			</p>

			<h2 id="your-repository">Your configuration, and your Git repository</h2>
			<p>
				Your configuration is stored in a <strong>Git repository</strong>. In plain terms, that is a
				folder of files that also remembers its own history: every change ever made to it, kept in
				order, and never silently overwritten.
			</p>
			<p>
				Each individual change is called a <strong>commit</strong>, and a commit records three
				things — what changed, when it changed, and <strong>who changed it, by name and email
				address</strong>. That last part is the point. It is how a change can be traced back to a
				real person months later, and it is why the history is worth keeping at all. We use it for
				nothing else: it is not analysed, not scored, and never shared outside your organisation.
			</p>

			<div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top:1.8rem">
				<div class="f"><span class="fi"><Icon name="shield" /></span>
					<div class="ft">If you own the repository</div>
					<div class="fd">The folder and its history are yours. You choose where it is hosted and who can read, clone, or fork it — and you are responsible for the personal data inside it, including keeping it secure and backed up.</div>
				</div>
				<div class="f"><span class="fi"><Icon name="users" /></span>
					<div class="ft">If you are making the changes</div>
					<div class="fd">Your name and email address sit in that history, and anyone with access to the repository can read them. If the owner makes the repository public, so is your name. The owner controls that record — usually your employer, not us.</div>
				</div>
			</div>

			<h3>We keep no long-term copy</h3>
			<p>
				<strong>The lasting copy of your configuration and its history is the repository owner's, not
				ours.</strong> We keep no separate archive of it. What we hold is a working copy — a checkout
				of the folder that the service needs in order to run, and to make the commits you asked us to
				make in your name. Nothing beyond what that requires.
			</p>
			<p>
				Those working copies are not persisted as a record of their own. <strong>When our access to
				your repository ends — you revoke it, or you stop using ConfigButler — we delete our
				copies.</strong>
			</p>
			<p>
				One consequence is worth being blunt about: <strong>we are not your backup.</strong> There is
				no archive to restore from on our side. Keeping your repository backed up is the owner's
				responsibility, and it should not be us.
			</p>

			<h3>Removing your name and email</h3>
			<p>
				Because the history belongs to the repository owner, <strong>they are the ones who can remove
				personal data from it</strong>. Start there. In most cases it is genuinely not ours to
				rewrite, and that is exactly what makes the record trustworthy — a history we could quietly
				edit on request would not be worth keeping.
			</p>
			<p>
				Tell us as well, at
				<a class="link" href="mailto:simon@configbutler.ai">simon@configbutler.ai</a>. We will do
				everything we can: removing your name and email address ourselves where the owner asks us to
				and it is technically possible, helping them do it where it isn't, and telling you plainly if
				it cannot be done.
			</p>

			<h2>What we use it for</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>To reply to you when you get in touch.</li>
				<li>To authenticate you and run your account, if you have one.</li>
				<li>To attribute each configuration change to the person who made it.</li>
				<li>To keep the service working and secure.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				We do not use your information to build advertising profiles, and we will not quietly
				repurpose it for something unrelated.
			</p>

			<h2>What we never do</h2>
			<p>
				<strong>We do not sell, rent, or trade your personal information — to anyone, for any
				price.</strong> We do not share it with advertisers, ad networks, or data brokers. We do not
				sell it as part of a "partnership", a "data collaboration", or any other rewording of the same
				thing. There is no version of this business where your personal data is the product.
			</p>

			<h2>Who we share it with</h2>
			<p>
				Only the small set of providers we need to operate, and only so they can do their job for us:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>GitHub Pages</strong> — hosts this website.</li>
				<li><strong>Web3Forms</strong> — delivers contact form submissions to our inbox.</li>
				<li><strong>LinkedIn</strong> and <strong>GitHub</strong> — handle sign-in.</li>
				<li><strong>Our email provider</strong> — carries the mail we exchange with you.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Beyond that, we do not hand your personal information to anyone unless we are legally compelled
				to.
			</p>
			<p>
				If ConfigButler is ever sold, merged, or acquired, personal data may transfer to the buyer as
				part of that — which is normal, and we would rather say so plainly than pretend otherwise.
				What we commit to is that the buyer is bound by the promises on this page, that we will tell
				account holders it has happened, and that you can delete your data if you would rather not
				continue.
			</p>

			<h2>Built and hosted in Europe</h2>
			<p>
				ConfigButler is a Dutch company and we build here on purpose. The service runs on European
				infrastructure, your configuration stays in the repository you chose, and anything we add
				later — measurement included — gets hosted in the EU as well. This is not a compliance
				checkbox for us. It is where we want our work to live.
			</p>
			<p>
				We would rather name the exceptions than claim a clean sweep. This marketing site is static
				and served by <strong>GitHub Pages</strong>, the contact form is delivered by
				<strong>Web3Forms</strong>, and the fonts load from <strong>Google Fonts</strong> — all
				outside the EU. None of them touch your configuration. They carry a public website and the
				message you chose to send us, and nothing else.
			</p>

			<h2>How long we keep it</h2>
			<p>
				Contact emails are kept while the conversation is useful, and deleted when it isn't. Account
				data is kept while your account exists and is removed when you delete it or ask us to.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Change history is the deliberate exception.</strong> The name and email recorded
				against a change stay in the Git history even after you close your account, because an audit
				trail that could be quietly rewritten would be worthless. That history is the repository
				owner's to keep or erase — see
				<a class="link" href="#your-repository">your configuration, and your Git repository</a>.
			</p>

			<h2>Your rights</h2>
			<p>
				Under the GDPR you can ask us to give you a copy of your data, correct it, export it, delete
				it, or stop using it. Send one email to
				<a class="link" href="mailto:simon@configbutler.ai">simon@configbutler.ai</a> and we will sort
				it out within 30 days, usually a lot sooner. You also have the right to complain to your local
				data protection authority — in the Netherlands, the Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens.
			</p>

			<h2>Cookies and measurement</h2>
			<p>
				<strong>Today this website sets no cookies and runs no analytics at all.</strong> If you sign
				in to a ConfigButler application, we use a strictly necessary session cookie to keep you
				signed in — nothing more.
			</p>
			<p>
				That may change — knowing which pages get read and where an explanation loses people is
				genuinely useful, and we would like to see it. If we add measurement, it will be to make the
				product and this site better. It will not be used to build a profile of you to sell, and your
				behaviour will not be handed to advertising networks or data brokers. We will say so here when
				it changes.
			</p>

			<h2>Security</h2>
			<p>
				Everything is served over HTTPS, access to personal data is limited to people who need it, and
				we hold as little of it as possible — the most reliable way to protect data is not to have
				collected it.
			</p>
			<p>
				That covers the parts we run. Your own Git repository sits outside it: we can write an honest
				record into it, but we cannot protect a repository we do not control. Securing it is on you.
			</p>

			<h2>Children</h2>
			<p>
				ConfigButler is a tool for software teams and is not directed at anyone under 16. We do not
				knowingly collect their personal information.
			</p>

			<h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
			<p>
				If this policy changes, we update the date at the top of this page. Material changes that
				affect how we handle your data will be flagged to account holders by email.
			</p>

			<h2>Contact</h2>
			<p>
				Questions, requests, or concerns:
				<a class="link" href="mailto:simon@configbutler.ai">simon@configbutler.ai</a>.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</Layout>
